   138	!! The session-scoped document view ~ what replaces the broad sync port
   139	
   140	''A correctly routed holder protects nothing once an island can fetch any document.'' So a session island never receives unrestricted Repo sync. It receives a ''session-scoped document view'', and that view gates five things: document announcements, fetches, writes, catalog resolution, and content-addressed reads.
   141	
   142	''Session islands hold ephemeral replicas; the vessel's owner alone persists.'' No durable per-island store survives a session. A plane address a caller supplies grants nothing: the view answers from the session's own facets.
   143	
   144	''The first test of this contract'': ask a session island's sync path for an arbitrary document. One byte of another face's plane, or one catalog name from it, breaks the contract.
   145	
   146	''The view rides the carrier.'' A peer names its own id, so a verdict keyed by peer id protects nothing from an island that announces another's. The view mints the island's peer id and judges every message that crosses the island's own port, in both directions; an island that announces any other id loses its channel. A fetch the view refuses answers as absence does, so a refusal tells an island nothing about what stands.
   147	
   148	> ''Four house readings, marked as such.'' ''A bag reaches a session read-only'': a session writes its wiki's working, personal and draft layers and its face's own planes, and a move into a bag runs as an owner-side verb, never as an island's sync write. ''One table of profile against layer decides a write'': a seat writes the working layer alone, and the watcher writes the working layer and surfaces drafts. ''Content opens by reference'': a session reads the content its recipe names and the content a document it may read references; the owner's store answers, and bytes that fail to recompute to their address never leave. ''An island settles before it goes'': a session island keeps no store, so at an orderly close it names the heads it holds and waits until the owner holds them; a switch or a revocation closes its channel first.
