/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/field-collision.mem:35:''SPKI/SDSI (Rivest & Lampson 1996, RFC 2693) and KERI (Smith 2019) converged INDEPENDENTLY on our exact ontology.'' SDSI: names read LOCALLY, authority a KEY not a registry, a principal exports bindings ("Joe's poker-buddies") — identity-as-relation, standardized 27 years ago. KERI: self-certifying identifier · key-STATE as presenting authority · succession by GRAFTING the key-state (chained signed KEL) · identifier fixed for life · duplicity caught WITHOUT a clock. ''Two generations of security researchers reached the name-is-an-edge, the-authority-is-a-rolling-key-state, the-identifier-is-permanent-while-holders-are-not — from first principles.'' The field arrives at this model when it takes decentralization seriously. Caution kept: SDSI LOST the market to X.509's global CAs — the better naming theory does not win on merit.
/home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine/bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof.mem:295:''A signer serves one session at one generation.'' It mints at random when the session opens, its certificate carries no session id and no owner epoch, and it drops when the session closes or switches. It asks the owner's own table before every signature and again before a signature leaves, so a record kept from before a close proves nothing and a close that lands mid-signature releases none.
